{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 9f61e3c1: a port function is supported for its call only.\n\nd66ce570 and 654ec50a cover data: each value, descriptor and key that\na rule reads from its Given. They do not require function metadata to\ncorrespond.\n\n1. A method of the public view, and each other function that Explore\n   hands to a rule, is a callable port. The supported observation is\n   the call: its arguments, its result and its throw. Those equal the\n   runtime's, or the call is unavailable.\n2. The identity of a port function, its name, its length, its text\n   from toString and its own properties are outside the supported\n   observation. Explore gives no promise for them and builds no guard\n   for them. JavaScript cannot give an adapter the identity of the\n   original, so a promise there would be false.\n3. Add no adapter, no Proxy on a function and no unavailable answer\n   for a metadata read. Copy no name and no length onto a\n   replacement, because a partial copy reads as a promise.\n4. The rules are dap's code in packages/derive and packages/platform.\n   No definition supplies JavaScript. So the boundary holds by review.\n   State in the checkpoint, from a source search, that no capability\n   rule and no platform rule reads a property of a view method or\n   compares a method's identity. List each site if one exists.\n5. No new witness. The existing cases that call the view through the\n   guard and compare the answers with the runtime's stand.\n\nI add this boundary to section 5.4 of the note in the amendment after\nE1, with the rule that a kernel review refuses a rule which reads\nfunction metadata of a port.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}